  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 258 AM EDT Sun Sep 6 2026\n\n- Dry through Labor Day\n\nIR loop shows clear skies across the region as high pressure moves\noverhead. A light east wind is advecting drier air in from Ontario\nand we'll see humidity levels lower today, along with cooler\ntemperatures; highs will be in the upper 70s. Low temperatures\ntonight will fall into the mid 40s in the northeast cwa and lower\nto mid 50s elsewhere. A building high over the central US will \nbegin to drift east on Labor Day and temperatures will rise a few\ndegrees, but still remain near normal highs in the upper 70s.\n\n- Chance of showers/storms Tuesday night/Wednesday\n\nThe high will continue to be nudged eastward and flattened aloft\nas an upper short wave pushes a cold front into the western Great\nLakes. We're beginning to get into the time of year that cold\nfronts from the west have stronger support aloft due to higher\ntemperature gradients between southern Canada and the northern\nhalf of the US and thus create more lift as they collide with the\nwarmer, more humid airmass to the south. The models show this\noccurring Tuesday night as a cold front moves south into Lower MI.\nPrecipitation chances rise to 70-80 percent Tuesday night and\nWednesday morning. Another half inch of rain looks possible as the\nfront moves through. \n\nThe latter half of the week looks dry as high pressure builds into\nthe Great Lakes. Highs in the upper 70s will be common Wednesday\nthrough Friday.",
